<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">River Great Ouse</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The River Great Ouse is a river in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, about 230 km long, flowing from Northamptonshire through Bedford, Huntingdon and Ely to the Wash at King's Lynn. The treaty of around 886 between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-the-great">Alfred the Great</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guthrum">Guthrum</a> used it as part of the boundary between English and Danish territory. Its course through the Fens has been heavily drained and engineered.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/river-great-ouse.jpg" alt="River Great Ouse" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was William M. Connolley at English Wikipedia. · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:DSCN1566-goba-mooring-after-brownshill-staunch_1200x900.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Great Ouse flows mostly north and east from Syresham in Northamptonshire through Buckinghamshire, Bedfordshire, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridgeshire">Cambridgeshire</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norfolk">Norfolk</a>, before draining into the Wash near King's Lynn. Authorities disagree about both its source and its length. Its best-known tributary is the Cam, which runs through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>. The river passes Buckingham, Bedford, St Neots, Huntingdon and St Ives, and at Earith it enters a short tidal section before the artificial Old and New Bedford rivers carry water north-east towards Denver. The name Ouse comes from a Celtic or pre-Celtic word that probably means simply 'water'.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In prehistory the river flowed from Huntingdon straight to Wisbech and the sea. Its lower reaches silted repeatedly, and in times of flood the waters breached neighbouring watersheds and made new courses, generally progressively eastward. After major floods in the early 13th century it breached another watershed near Denver and took over the channel of the old Wiggenhall Eau, so that it joined the Wash at King's Lynn. The upper course, from Bedford to Stony Stratford, marked the boundary between English territory and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danelaw">Danelaw</a> under the Treaty of Alfred and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guthrum">Guthrum</a>.</p><p>The Land Drainage Act 1601 allowed 'adventurers', who paid for drainage schemes themselves, to be repaid in land. Below Earith, thirteen Adventurers working with the Earl of Bedford formed a corporation to drain the Bedford Levels, with Cornelius Vermuyden as engineer. The Old Bedford River, a straight cut from Earith to a new sluice near Salters Lode, was completed in 1637, and the New Bedford River, authorised by an act of 29 May 1649, in 1652. Ports and towns on the river strongly opposed the works, and there were attempts to destroy them during the English civil wars. Above Earith, navigation was extended to Bedford in 1689.</p><p>The sluice at Denver failed in 1713 and was rebuilt by 1750. With the coming of the railways the river declined and its navigation was declared derelict in the 1870s. Responsibility was divided among many bodies until the Great Ouse Catchment Board was created under the Land Drainage Act 1930.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Ouse Washes, between the Old and New Bedford rivers, are the largest area of washland in the United Kingdom: pasture in summer that floods in winter, a breeding ground for lapwings, redshanks and snipe, and a winter home of ducks and swans. The river is navigable from the Wash to Kempston Mill near Bedford, 72 miles with 17 locks.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “River Great Ouse” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 879 his army settled East Anglia, the former Kingdom of East Anglia, and a treaty with Alfred around 886 fixed the boundary along the Lea, the <b>Ouse</b> and Watling Street.</blockquote><a href="/state/danish-kingdom-of-east-anglia">Danish Kingdom of East Anglia</a></li></ul></section>
